# Dev guidelines

How to navigate and extend this codebase. A fresh contributor should read this
file before adding code, so that new contributions land where the rest of the
project would expect them. This file is jointly maintained by `/refactor`
(style + idioms + anti-patterns) and `/unspaghettify` (file roles + dependency
direction + placement); each command MERGES into its own sections without
clobbering the other's.

## Module roles (one sentence each)

Grouped by layer, top of the dependency graph last.

### Leaves — depend on nothing in-project

- `launch/utils.py` — domain-neutral helpers (formatting, sorting, parsing, host:port split, user prompts).
- `launch/paths.py` — every host + container path the launcher knows; the *only* place paths get defined. Constants + path-builder lambdas only — no disk listings (those are file_access work). Its ONE in-project import is the Claude adapter record (`launch/ai/claude_code.py`), for the config root's dir and file names — bound at import, so paths stay Claude-bound until the switch turns them into functions of `active_harness()`.
- `launch/template_code/` — pure data, no logic: docker-side strings (`docker_prompts.py`), the curated firewall domain list (`firewall_domains.py` — CDN provider ranges are NOT data here or anywhere in source; the firewall resolver fetches them live).
- `launch/toml_emit.py` — rendering the TOML the launcher writes by hand: key quoting, basic-string escaping, the `axis = [...]` line. Reading TOML is `tomllib`'s job; both hand-written stores (`tags/store.py`, `cluster/state.py`) take their escaping from here while each still owns its own shape.
- `launch/container_probe.py` — one named, LIVE container: how it is addressed (`CONTAINER_NAME_PREFIX`) and how to interrogate it (probe, wait, read its tty size, exec as root). Fleet-wide "what is running" queries stay in `docker_config`.
- `launch/container_inject.py` — the write-side twin: typing a prompt into a live session's TTY (`docker_attach_inject`, the `{cowork}` doorbell). Owns the pty/termios plumbing nothing else in the tree needs.
- `launch/ai/` — the CODE half of "which harness runs": `catalog.py` (`DEFAULT_HARNESS_KEY`, the call-time `active_harness_key()` / `set_active_harness()` every consumer reads — never a module constant computed from it, the import-time trap) and the adapters' data half (`adapter.py` — `Adapter`, the record of an agent CLI's names: binary, flags, config-root files, env vars, critical hosts; `claude_code.py` — the one instance; `ADAPTERS` keyed by the harness member's name, `adapter_for()` / `active_adapter()` in `__init__` — a LookupError for a harness without an adapter, never Claude Code's names borrowed; `refusal_for()` / `adopt()` for the launch paths). The AIs and the harnesses themselves are TAG MEMBERS (`agents/ai/<key>/`, `tags/ai.py`; `agents/harness/<key>/`, `tags/harness.py`): vendor, colours, the tiers per capability standard, the AIs each CLI runs and each CLI's settings vocabulary (`knobs.mapping`) are tree data. A leaf on purpose: `paths.py` derives the container's config-root names from the Claude Code adapter; a test forbids any production module outside `ai/` spelling those names.

### Disk — depends only on leaves

- `launch/file_access.py` — sole chokepoint for every disk-touching call (reads, atomic writes, removes, queries, lists, stats) — including the agents/ name → md-path index (`agent_md_index`, cached).

### Disk consumers — depend on file_access + leaves

- `launch/transcripts.py` — reading Claude Code's own session records: is there anything `--continue` could load and how big is it, when was this instance last used, what was said last and by whom. Encodes the SHAPE of a format that is not ours (which turn types count, that a `tool_result` is typed "user" without being a human prompt, that sidechains belong to somebody else) — and degrades every parse to None rather than raising, so an upstream schema change shows up as an empty preview, not a failed launch.

### The tag system — `launch/tags/`, depends on disk + leaves

- `tags/base.py` — the `Tag` record, `DockerContribution`, `tag.info`/`tag.docker` parsing, the STRICT tree rule, `TagError`.
- `tags/ai.py`, `tags/harness.py`, `tags/engine.py`, `tags/profession.py`, `tags/specialty.py`, `tags/policy.py` — the six kind classes, each with its own `scan(agents_dir)`. `tags/budget.py` is the engine budget vocabulary (`Budget` from `tag.budget`; the capability-standard spelling and order — `is_standard` / `rank_of`: `cheapest`, dated `YYYYQn` quarters, `best`; the switches and amounts) both `ai.py` and `engine.py` import: an engine states its budget in the launcher's words, `Harness.render(budget, ai)` turns it, with the AI's tier, into one CLI's native settings (the adapter boundary for settings), and `Instance.conf` is that rendering for the instance's harness and AI. Engine ORDER is the budget's standard rank (`standard_rank`, `sorted_engines`) — AI-neutral; the Claude-shaped family heuristic retired 2026-09-13. `tags/engine.py` once also owned the model-family sort keys (`engine_sort_key`), profession discovers hidden `_<name>` image layers, specialty adds `combos.info`, policy adds `merge_fragments`.
- `tags/registry.py` — `scan_all(agents_dir)` → `Registry`: discover, cross-validate (fail loud), look up.
- `tags/lego.py` — `AgentBuild` + `.lego` loading (an agent's default tag selections). Shipped `.lego` files are validated with the RAISING `Registry.validate_build`; user-editable `instances.toml` entries use the NON-raising `Registry.resolve_store_build` (keeps valid tags, returns `TagProblem`s for the rest) so a stale/typo'd stored tag becomes a blocked, flagged instance rather than a crash.
- `tags/toolkit_profile.py` — per-profession `~/.ai-agents/<profession>_profile.toml` load/generate/save (install toggles for a profession's optional `template.form` entries); depends on `tags/profession.py`'s `ToolkitEntry`.
- `tags/identity.py` — `Agent` (pickable) and `Instance` (fully-resolved launch: `chain`, `build_steps`, `docker_contributions`, `conf`, `claude_args`, `unmet_wants`); and `label_error`, the ONE legality rule every name a person types is held to (an instance's session, a cluster's session, a member's role) — returned in the form validator's shape so the field shows it live, and wrapped by `cluster.member.valid_label` / `cowork.group._legal_label` in their own exceptions.
- `tags/store.py` — `instances.toml` load/save (stdlib `tomllib` in; a small fixed-schema TOML emitter out).
- `tags/migrations.py` — ISOLATED one-shot conversions from retired on-disk formats; the only module allowed to know retired formats exist.
- `tags/addendums.py` — the CLAUDE.md addendum composer: universal BASE_ADDENDUMS, the placeholder registry, and `compose(active_tags)`; per-tag copy lives in each tag.info's `[addendum]` table.

### Small services — depend on disk + tags

- `launch/claude_code_config.py` — everything the launcher RENDERS about an instance: the in-container status line + terminal title, and the pre-build launch banner (`print_launch_banner`, printed by run.py).
- `launch/firewall/` — the `{firewall}` outbound-whitelist subsystem, packaged (like `tags/` and `gui/`); `__init__.py` is the public facade (five entry points), and consumers import from `launch.firewall`, never the submodules:
  - `resolver.py` — the coordinator: the coupled core that stays one module because it shares sequenced, in-place-mutated, lock-free state — the widening POLICY, host-side DNS cascade (`getent ahostsv4` on Linux, `socket.getaddrinfo` fallback on hosts without getent, e.g. macOS), cross-launch IP cache (fresh-DNS union / failure fallback — never a substitute for a live lookup), burst-batched iptables updater (gated on init-firewall.sh's completion marker so it never races the in-container setup), and the post-launch refresher daemon that re-resolves the whitelist every few minutes to heal DNS drift. Critical Anthropic pins are widened to the registered `160.79.104.0/21` block, and `selftest_address()` feeds init-firewall.sh's DNS-free self-test (`$1` → `curl --resolve`); the script also probes/reorders dead-first nameservers in resolv.conf at startup. The test suite (`test_firewall.py`) patches/reads this module directly — it's the single namespace the coordinator reads its collaborators from.
  - `cdn_ranges.py` — the fetched-and-cached table of provider IPv4 blocks the resolver widens against (`load()` once per launch, `provider_ranges(ips)` per resolution, `blocks_for(provider)` for a wildcard's whole-edge grant). Knows nothing of DNS, phases or iptables; caches per provider under `FIREWALL_CACHE_DIR` with a graceful chain (fresh cache → live fetch → stale cache + warning → provider skipped).
  - `iptables.py` — what an opened address MEANS in iptables (`rules_for`: two rules per address by default, inserted before the catch-all REJECT) and how a batch reaches a live container (`flush`). The token-validation security boundary lives here: a token failing the strict address/port shape is DROPPED, never escaped, because these strings are joined into a `sh -c` script that runs as root.
  - `whitelist.py` — the pure raw-entry → work-item expansion (wildcard + IPv6-skip aware; `HostnameEntry` / `_expand_whitelist` / `_index_by_host`); no DNS, threads, or disk, so the security-relevant parsing is unit-testable in isolation.
  - `status.py` — the lock-guarded `domains_pending_resolve.yml` tracker (`_WhitelistResolutionStatus` + the process-wide `_status` singleton) that mirrors DNS-resolution progress to the agent-visible status file.

### Orchestration — depend on services

- `launch/container_env.py` — env staging (`ContainerEnvKey` taxonomy, the staged-value accumulator, `-e`/build-arg formatters, `set_container_env` orchestrator, `toolkit_install_flags` — profile-driven toolchain INSTALL_<TOOL> flags — and `install_creds_flags` — creds-driven CLI flags; disjoint key sets).
- `launch/docker_config.py` — plain-docker orchestration: `ensure_image` (base + per-layer `docker build`), `run_container` (`docker run` assembly), tag.docker flag emitters, bind-mount accumulator, docker CLI wrappers, the dry-run gate.
- `launch/tag_handlers.py` — `apply_tags(instance)`: stages declarative tag.docker mounts, then dispatches per-tag `_apply_<name>` handlers (dynamic-only side effects; data-only tags have no handler).
- `launch/agents_crud.py` — instance state CRUD (instances.toml writers), state-dir installs (`install_latest_md`, `install_settings`), `resolve_pick`, `compute_resume_flag` (Instance → `["--continue"]` | `[]`, shared by run.py + quickie), picker-entry factories.
- `launch/user_additions.py` — user-side contributions to the container (optional-creds mounts + first-launch template plants).
- `launch/audit.py` — read-only state-correctness checker, runnable as `python -m launch.audit`.

### UI — `launch/gui/`, depends on tags + services

Nine modules, nothing outside the package imports prompt_toolkit, and run.py
drives everything through `launch/gui/__init__.py`'s re-exports. Direction
inside is strictly one-way: `styles` → `form_core` → {`forms`,
`cluster_form`} → {`picker_previews`, `picker_prompts`} → {`picker_flows`,
`picker_widget`} → `menu_picker`.

- `gui/styles.py` — the style system every surface draws with: the `UiClass` CSS classes, the tag colours (`tag_style` — per kind, and per MEMBER for AIs, whose tag.info paints them; `squashed_tag_style`; `RICH_BY_STYLE` + `rich_style`, the prompt_toolkit → rich conversion that keeps a tag one colour in rows and panes), the display coercers, and the chrome heights (`TITLE_HEIGHT` / `STATUS_HEIGHT`) forms and picker both reserve.
- `gui/form_core.py` — the generic form: `FormOption`, `TextField`, the shared confirm/really-done? gate (`ConfirmGate` + `confirm_gate`), and `run_form` + `FormBody` — the ONE scaffold every full-screen form runs on (text fields, cursor, key map, warning window, layout), with `checkbox_form` as the multi-select row model on it. No form may restate any of it: the two forms drifted twice while each had its own copy (plans/ISSUES.md, testing technique).
- `gui/forms.py` — what the launcher ASKS: the instance tag form (`prompt_tags` — the ⟪AI⟫ and ⟦harness⟧ radio groups first, then the engines, then the checkbox kinds; `_harness_warnings` lights the red zone for a pair that cannot run together, and the build then falls back to the AI's own harness), its cluster-wide sibling (none of the radio groups: all three are per member), the merged preferences form (`edit_profiles_menu`).
- `gui/cluster_form.py` — the membership form: its agent rows, its add/remove ROW actions (picking an agent adds ANOTHER) and the members panel, declared as a `FormBody` on `run_form`; its pick algebra comes from `cluster.legoset`.
- `gui/picker_previews.py` — `session_preview`, the ONE pane instances, cluster members and clusters render through (a fact block, the optional `Last prompt`, the expanded tag list — the AI first, its harness second), the creation rows' panes (the Create pane shows the persona only — an AI is chosen per instance, not per agent), and the child-process transcript read that keeps a huge transcript off the UI thread.
- `gui/picker_prompts.py` — the non-fullscreen bits both the picker and its flows need: line prompts (workspace, session suffix), inline dialogs, field builders, and the confirm wording every one of them shares.
- `gui/picker_flows.py` — what each picker key MEANS once a row is chosen: the create / edit / destroy flows.
- `gui/picker_widget.py` — the REUSABLE picker: the row contract (`PickerEntry` with its `marker`, its per-key gates — `deletable` / `modifiable` / `pickable` — and deferred-preview slots; `ContEntry` for anything with a state dir and `MemberEntry`, its cluster-member subclass; `WorkspaceView`, a row's path + cwd hint as one fact), the row decorations whose glyphs+styles are data on an enum rather than branches in a renderer (`PickerRowMarker`, `PickerCwdHint`), the preview LOADER, cursor movement over skippable rows, the accent bar's colour (read off the row's marker) and the selection loop (`pick_with_preview`). Knows nothing about agents.
- `gui/menu_picker.py` — the launcher's three MENUS built on that widget: the main agent picker (`select_agent`), the deletion submenu, `--stop`'s selector (`prompt_stop`), plus the F8 composition legend — and the row DATA they share: `_CwdContext` (the launch-site facts a workspace is judged against, resolved once per menu build), the two row factories (`continuable_instances`, `cluster_entries` — whose members are Instances via `Cluster.member_instance`), and `_session_row`, the one anatomy every row for an existing thing wears (tags · `_runtime_column` — the AI, then its harness · name · hints · path — the runtime cell padded per population like the tags). This is the only gui module with agent-domain knowledge. (Launch-stage output like the pre-build banner is NOT here — that's claude_code_config, which owns everything the launcher renders about an instance.)

### Clusters — `launch/cluster/`, a leaf consumer of the core

N members, one container, one project. Like `cowork/` and `quickie/`, nothing
in `launch/` imports it back except `run.py`'s launch call.

- `cluster/member.py` — what one member IS; id composition; `valid_label` — `tags.identity.label_error` as a `ClusterError`, plus the member-id separator rule — which both backends and `panes` enforce.
- `cluster/legoset.py` — `.legoset` parsing: a cluster template's default membership.
- `cluster/state.py` — `cluster.toml`: durable member set, the cluster-level tags every member inherits, and discovery-by-scan; plus what a member IS at launch (`Cluster.member_instance` — the ordinary `Instance`, non-raising: a vanished agent is `None`, a stale tag lands on `invalid_tags`) and when the cluster was last used (`last_used_mtime`, its newest member). Its `dumps` owns this format's shape; escaping comes from `toml_emit`.
- `cluster/panes.py` — what a multiplexer is asked to start: one `Pane` per member (name, argv, cwd, env) plus the two labels neither backend owns. The vocabulary BOTH backends read — deliberately free of either one's flag dialect.
- `cluster/tmux.py` — the tmux backend: pure argv/script assembly plus the key/status policy.
- `cluster/herdr.py` — the herdr backend, selected by default; same assembly-only contract in its own dialect.
- `cluster/worktree.py` — writer safety: one git worktree per member (argv assembly + probes).
- `cluster/launch_plan.py` — the join: a `Cluster` + env → worktrees, panes, mounts.
- `cluster/launching.py` — the integration step: state on disk → one running container with N members, each a full Claude session in its own window/tab.
- `cluster/solo.py` — what `{muxer}` does to a SOLO launch: the same two backends, one agent.
- `cluster/cli.py` — the operator surface (`cluster.py` is its repo-root entry).

### The cluster work protocol — `launch/cluster_work_protocol/`

The message queue cluster members speak on, mounted INTO the container and
run there as `cluster-chat`. Standalone by constraint: stdlib +
intra-package imports only, because in the container there is no `launch/`.

- `schema.py` — the message shape and reply vocabulary (including the fold rule: a `nop` carries no data).
- `queue.py` — the only module that touches `chat.jsonl` (flock'd appends, total order, per-member cursors).
- `gates.py` — the iteration machinery: open, reply accounting, nudge/timeout, close. All state is derived from the journal.
- `wake.py` — the ping side: how a queue event WAKES a member.
- `config.py` — every tunable, loaded from `settings/cluster_protocol.toml`.
- `cli.py` — the `cluster-chat` command members actually call.

### Entry

- `run.py` — staged `launch()` orchestrator (scan tags → migrate store → input → fs-validate → resume → persist → apply tags → setup → build → run).
- `quick_question.py` — thin repo-root entry for the quickie tool (the `q` alias target): hands argv to `launch.quickie.main` (argparse, so `q -h` is quickie's own help, not claude's).
- `launch/quickie/` — the `q` one-shot-question tool: a LEAF CONSUMER of the launcher core (imports `launch/` helpers; nothing in `launch/` imports it back). Layers: `cli.py` = the argparse front end (a question, `--explain`/`--research` to pick the agent, `--history`/`--answer <id>`/`--resume <id>` thread ops) dispatching to → `ask.py`, a lean cousin of `run.py`'s `launch()` (no picker/form/store) that builds an Instance from a `QuickieAgent` spec — QUICK (`_quickie`, quick/Sonnet, default), TRIVIA (`_trivia`, reliable/Opus, `--explain`), RESEARCH (`researcher.md` + `_research.lego`, researcher engine, base image, `--research`) — under `quickie/<gibberish>/` (via `Instance.state_dir_override`) with the shared `quickie/communal/` workspace and runs `claude -p` in stream-json mode (via `run_container(interactive=False, print_prompt=…, stream_renderer=render_stream)`), reusing the core's `compute_resume_flag` for `--resume`'s `--continue`; `render.py` = `render_stream`, which turns Claude Code's stream-json events into a live `⋯ thinking…` stderr ticker + a streamed stdout answer (reasoning TEXT is redacted by current models — `display: omitted` — so only elapsed time is shown, never the chain-of-thought); and `history.py` = `--history` (grey timestamp + id + last question, ascending) and `--answer <id>` (reprint the saved answer), via `transcripts.last_prompt_in_state` / `last_answer_in_state`. Shared mechanisms it needs live in the core (the override field, run_container print mode + its `stream_renderer` hook / `docker_stream_subprocess`, compute_resume_flag, the transcript reads), never in quickie.

- `cowork.py` — thin repo-root entry for the multi-agent hub CLI (mirrors `quick_question.py`): hands argv to `launch.cowork.main`.
- `launch/cowork/` — multi-agent group hosting: like quickie, a LEAF CONSUMER of the core (nothing in `launch/` imports it back except `run.py`'s one `ensure_hub_running` call and `audit.py`'s checks). One concern per module — `group` (durable state + discovery-by-scan), `mailbox` (message plane + capture attribution), `sync` (file plane), `journal` (conversation log), `roster` (who is recruitable), `control` (agent-facing verbs, tag-gated), `lifecycle` (singleton + start/stop policy), `relay` (the loop + routing policy; orchestration only), `cli` (argparse). It owns NO docker calls: injection comes from `container_inject` (its only writer to a container) and the liveness snapshot from `docker_config`'s fleet query, in `roster`. Dependency direction inside: `relay` never imports `control` (control calls `relay.send`) — the CLI wires them together via `serve(also_poll=…)`.

