- `launch/template_files/` — source-side files `file_access.copy_file` plants to `user_extras/` on first launch.
- `launch/tests/` — unittest suite (`python3 -m unittest discover -s launch/tests` from project root).
- `agents/` — agent personas (`<name>.md` + `<name>.lego`) and the tag tree (`engine/`, `profession/`, `specialty/`, `policy/`).
- `Dockerfile` (repo root) — the base image; per-layer Dockerfiles live in the tag tree next to their tags.
- `settings/` — in-container bashrc + status line + manifest helpers + base Claude settings/keybindings (bind-mounted via `DOCKER_BASE_MOUNTS`; `settings.json` is merged with policy fragments per instance rather than mounted directly).
- `custom_commands/` — launcher-bundled slash commands (bind-mounted into every container).
- `custom_skills/` — launcher-bundled skills (bind-mounted as a single dir; each `<name>/SKILL.md` becomes a `/<name>` skill).

## Dependency direction

The arrows must stay one-way. A reversal creates a cycle (or hides one behind
lazy imports, which is the bandage of last resort).

```
leaves: utils, paths, template_code/, toml_emit, container_probe, ai/
        (independent — none imports in-project;
         container_inject imports only container_probe)
                  │
                  ▼
            file_access ──► transcripts
                  │
                  ▼
                tags/ ──┐
                        ├─► small services (claude_code_config, firewall/)
                        │
      small services ──► orchestration (container_env, docker_config, tag_handlers,
                                        agents_crud, user_additions, audit)
                                 │
                                 ├─► gui/   (styles ─► form_core ─► forms ─►
                                 │           previews/prompts ─► flows/widget
                                 │           ─► menu_picker)
                                 │
                                 ├─► leaf consumers: cluster/, cowork/, quickie/
                                 │   (nothing in launch/ imports these back)
                                 │
                                 ▼
                                 entry (run.py, cluster.py, cowork.py,
                                        quick_question.py, -m launch.audit)
```

**NO import cycles (since 2026-09-03).** There used to be exactly one —
`docker_config ↔ firewall`: `docker_config` imports the `launch.firewall`
facade at module level (`is_critical_pending` / `start_firewall_updater` /
`wait_for_critical_addresses`), while `firewall.resolver` needed four
container probes back out of `docker_config` and got them with LAZY
`from ..docker_config import …` calls inside two function bodies.

It was fixed the way this guide already prescribed — "push the shared logic
to a leaf-er module first": those four calls now live in
`launch/container_probe.py`, which imports only `paths`, `utils` and the
stdlib, so both sides import DOWNWARD and the lazy imports are gone. The
same pass deleted the tree's only `TYPE_CHECKING` guard
(`cluster/state.py` deferring `Registry`), which was guarding against a
cycle that did not exist — `tags/` has never imported `cluster/`.

So the rule is now plain: **no lazy (function-body) imports and no
`TYPE_CHECKING` guards for cycle-breaking.** If two modules need each other,
the shared part belongs in a third, leaf-er one — `container_probe` is the
worked example, and its docstring states the line it draws so the next
addition knows whether it belongs there.

The rule is machine-enforced, not honour-system: `TestNoImportCycles`
(`test_essential_files.py`) parses every module's top-level imports into a
graph and fails on any back edge, naming the path that closes it. Lazy
imports are out of its scope by design — a name resolved on first call cannot
deadlock initialisation — so the two that remain (`cluster/cli.py` and
`docker_config`'s branch-local `cluster.solo`) are there for startup cost and
stay legal. Rewriting one as a top-level import is fine as long as the gate
stays green.

**`launch/benchmark/` is the one tree the gate does not run.** ruff and mypy
read it; nothing executes it; and a `patch("dotted.string")` target is
invisible to all three checks, so a rename leaves a benchmark broken and
quiet. `TestBenchmarksStayRunnable` imports each module and resolves each
patch target for that reason — when you move a function a benchmark patches,
that test is what tells you. Keep patch targets pointed at the module that
USES the name (`launch.firewall.resolver.wait_for_container_running`), not
the one that defines it: a module-level import binds the reference before any
patch runs, so patching the definer's module changes nothing.

## Where new code goes — by example

For each common addition, place it where the rest of the project would expect.
"X belongs in Y because Z" — the *because* matters more than the rule.

### A new tag (any kind)

→ a folder in the `agents/` tree — no launcher code:

- **AI**: `agents/ai/<name>/{tag.info, efforts.tiers}` — the manifest adds
  `vendor`, `harness` (the key of its default `agents/harness/` member — the
  registry checks it exists and runs this AI), `default` (exactly one member)
  and hex colours `fg` / `bg`; `efforts.tiers` answers every capability
  standard — `cheapest`, the dated quarters of the kind's shared
  `agents/ai/capability.standards` (`YYYYQn`: the quarter a model raised the
  frontier's AA index; `set_by`, `index`, `estimated`; indices must rise),
  `best` — with this AI's `model` + `effort` (with `[scale]`, the AI's effort
  words). Members do not nest; both files are required.
- **Harness**: `agents/harness/<name>/{tag.info, knobs.mapping}` — the manifest
  adds `vendor`, `ais` (the AI members this CLI runs — a build pairing it with
  another AI is refused for a `.lego`, and dropped with an `incompatible`
  TagProblem for a stored instance), `binary` and `package`; `knobs.mapping`
  maps each budget purpose to the CLI's native settings as `{value}` templates
  (`{value/100}`, `{value*4}` convert units once; `{provider}` — in keys too —
  is the CLI's slug for the instance's AI from a `[providers]` table, which
  must cover every AI the CLI runs once a template uses it). A purpose the CLI
  cannot express is left out and reported as unmapped at render time
  (`Harness.render(budget, ai)`; the mapping lived in the AI's dir until
  2026-09-14). Members do not nest. An instance's harness resolves to its
  build's, else its AI's default (`Registry.harness_for`). Seven members: the
  vendors' CLIs, one AI each, and OpenCode / OpenClaw / Hermes, which run all
  four. Only `claude-code` has an adapter in `launch/ai/`, so instances in the
  others describe, not launch.
- **Engine**: `agents/engine/<name>/{tag.info, tag.budget}` — the budget in the
  launcher's own words: `standard` (`cheapest`, `best`, or a quarter the AIs
  answer — the registry checks), the switches and the amounts
  (`tags/budget.py`); no AI's key names, ever. Nested folders overlay the
  parent's budget key-by-key. `tag.info` describes the TIER, never a model (a
  test forbids haiku / sonnet / opus / claude / gemini / gpt / grok in it): the
  form label, F8 legend, preview and banner render the model the AI runs for
  the step (`Instance.model`).
- **Profession**: `agents/profession/<name>/{tag.info, Dockerfile}` (+
  `tag.docker` naming the build-args the Dockerfile consumes). Nest under
  another profession to declare a requirement (`code/webdev/` ⇒ webdev requires code).
- **Specialty**: `agents/specialty/<name>/tag.info` (`short_description`,
  `full_description`, `warn`, `claude_args`, `workspace_readonly`, `[wants]`,
  `[addendum]`). May claim two hidden same-named assets: a `_<name>` image
  layer in the profession tree (→ `requires` + Dockerfile) and/or a
  `policy/_<name>/policy.json` settings fragment (merged like a policy). See
  `{ro}` for both-flavored (mount + fragment); `{dood}` for the layer. Image layer, if any: a hidden
  `_<name>/Dockerfile` under the profession it depends on. Static container
  config (mounts, `cap_add`, `entrypoint`, `env_forward`) goes in `tag.docker`.
  Multi-tag warnings go in `agents/specialty/combos.info`.
- **Policy**: `agents/policy/<name>/{tag.info, policy.json}` — a Claude Code
  settings fragment, merged per instance and mounted read-only; `always_on
  = true` makes it STATIC (merged into every instance by
  agents_crud.install_settings, locked in the form, rejected in .lego /
  silently dropped from store entries). `stance`
  (`allow`/`deny`/`demand`) drives the orange/blue/white coloring; the
  shortname convention mirrors it (`+` grants, `-` restricts, `!` demands).

Only add an `_apply_<name>` handler in `tag_handlers.py` when the tag needs
*dynamic* host-side work (cache pruning, GID detection, DNS kickoff) that
declarative `tag.docker` data can't express; tags without a handler are
legal no-ops. If the tag should inject launch-time CLAUDE.md guidance, add
an `Addendum` in `tags/addendums.py` and key it under the tag's name in
`ADDENDUMS_BY_TAG`.

### A new host or container path

→ `paths.py`. Because every path constant in the project lives here — search
finds it once, edit-via-relocation is one line. Static composite paths go
inline with their constituents; dynamic paths get a `<name>_path` lambda at
the bottom.

**If tests will ever redirect the path's ROOT, make it a no-arg BUILDER, not a
constant** (`group_hosting_dir()`, `instances_dir()` — lambdas reading
`AGENTS_STATE` at call time). A module that imports a constant binds it at
import, so patching `paths.AGENTS_STATE` silently leaves that module writing to
the real state dir — this bit twice: every cowork test fixture needed
per-module patches until the root became a builder, and store-writing tests
polluted the real `instances.toml` because `INSTANCES_FILE` is (still) an
import-time constant (they now patch `tags.store.INSTANCES_FILE` directly).

### A new bind-mount

→ `DOCKER_BASE_MOUNTS` in `paths.py` when every launch needs it; the owning
tag's `tag.docker` `[run] mounts` when it belongs to a tag (that's data, not
code — `apply_tags` stages every contribution generically); `CACHE_MOUNTS`
for `[code]` cache shares.

### A new disk-touching helper

→ `file_access.py`. Because that's the project's chokepoint — no other module
calls `open()`, `Path.exists()`, `shutil.*`, etc. The chokepoint rule is what
makes the rest of the codebase mock-able and keeps OS-quirk handling
(root-owned bind-mount leftovers, atomic writes) in one place.

### A new env var staged for the container

→ `container_env.py`. If it's a known static key, add it to the
`ContainerEnvKey` enum (flag `container_emit=True` for unconditional `-e`
emission) and stage via `stage_container_env(key, value)`. Build-args are
pulled by each layer's `tag.docker` `[build] arg_forward`; tag-conditional
run env by `[run] env_forward` — so a tag-scoped var usually needs the enum
entry + one line in the owning `tag.docker`, not new emission code. Dynamic
INSTALL_<TOOL> / token vars derive from `OPTIONAL_CREDS_MOUNTS` and
`OPTIONAL_CREDS_TOKEN_ENV_VARS` in `paths.py`.

### A new directory-contents lookup (like the agents/ name → md index)

→ `file_access.py`, as a cached function (see `agent_md_index`). Because a
directory *listing* is disk I/O — the chokepoint rule — even when the result
feels constant-like; `paths.py` stays declarations-only.

### A new launch-time CLAUDE.md directive

→ the owning tag's `tag.info`, as an `[addendum]` table (`title` + `body`);
`compose(active_tags)` renders it as a `### <title>` section while the tag
is active. Bodies may use the `{placeholder}` names published in
`tags/addendums.py` (validated at scan). A launcher-universal notice (no
owning tag) goes in `BASE_ADDENDUMS` in that module instead.

### A new picker row kind

→ split across the two picker modules, along the line they were divided on:
the row's DATA and the menu that offers it go in `gui/menu_picker.py`; a new
GLYPH or decoration goes on `picker_widget`'s `PickerRowMarker` /
`PickerCwdHint` enums (glyph + style as enum data, never a branch in the
renderer). Build entries with `pick_with_preview` — it is generic over
`PickerEntry.value` and must stay that way: if the widget needs to know what
kind of thing it is showing, the knowledge belongs on the row, not in the
loop. Concretely: give the row its `marker` (the accent bar reads it — the
one place the widget learns a row's KIND); if the thing has a state dir,
build it as a `ContEntry` (or a subclass, as `MemberEntry` is) so it gets
the deferred `Last used` / `Last prompt` pane for free; read its workspace
through `menu_picker._CwdContext.view` and render it with `_session_row`,
so its path lines up with every other row's and it wears the same hints.

### A new full-screen form

→ a row model + `FormBody` on `form_core.run_form` — never a prompt_toolkit
Application of its own. A form declares only what differs: its rows, which
keys act on a ROW (the scaffold already knows what every key does on a text
field and on the confirm button), its filler panel, its own complaints, its
confirm label and hint. The two forms each had their own Application until
2026-09-09 and drifted twice; `TestFormTailsMatch` now pins that only
`form_core` builds one, `TestFormPlacementParity` that complaints and the
cursor land where they should in both.

### A new thing to read out of a session transcript

→ `launch/transcripts.py`, not `file_access`. That module already owns the
format's quirks (turn types, tool-result echoes, sidechains) and the
degrade-to-None discipline every parse there follows. `file_access` stays the
chokepoint for the READ itself, which `transcripts` goes through.

### A new call that talks to a running container

→ `container_probe.py` if it interrogates one named container (and it may use
`CONTAINER_NAME_PREFIX`, which lives there); `container_inject.py` if it
writes to one's terminal; `docker_config.py` if it asks about the FLEET
("which instances are up"). The first two are leaves on purpose: the firewall
subsystem and the group-hosting layer both need them, and both would pull in
the whole build-and-run module otherwise.

### A new multiplexer backend, or a change to what a backend is asked to start

→ the backend's own module (`cluster/tmux.py`, `cluster/herdr.py`); the
REQUEST shape is `cluster/panes.py`. Nothing backend-specific may land in
`panes` — not a flag spelling, not a syntax. The test that pins this
(`test_cluster_panes.py`) asserts neither backend defines the record and that
`herdr` does not import `tmux`, because it did for months.

### A new state-correctness check

→ `audit.py`. Add a check function (the existing `_store_entry_issues`,
`_check_json_file` are unit-testable in isolation) and call it from `main()`.
Tests go in `launch/tests/test_audit.py`.

### A new identity property derived from existing fields

→ a `@property` on `Instance` (or `Agent`) in `tags/identity.py`. Don't add
a free function elsewhere — the dataclass owns its derivations. Same for any
"compute X from the instance" — it's `inst.x`, not `compute_x(inst)`.

### A new store-format change

→ the format itself lives in `tags/store.py`; the conversion FROM the
previous format goes in `tags/migrations.py` (the only module that may know
retired formats exist) as an extension of `ensure_migrated`.

### A third hand-written TOML store

→ its `dumps` lives with its own format (two similar shapes stay two — a
shared `dumps` would be parameterised by header, key set and value kinds
until it was harder to read than either caller). But its ESCAPING comes from
`toml_emit.py`, never a fresh `json.dumps` wrapper: `tags/store.py` and
`cluster/state.py` each carried a byte-identical copy of those five lines
until 2026-09-03, and the failure mode of letting them drift is silent —
each file's own reader keeps parsing until a key finally needs quoting.

### A new name a person types (a session, a label, a role)

→ hold it to `tags.identity.label_error` — as the field's `validate` in a
form, or wrapped in the feature's own exception at the point the name enters
durable state — and add only the feature's OWN separator rule on top (`__`
for member ids; cowork's `@` and `:` are in the shared table). Never a second
character table: three subsets of one rule drifted apart until 2026-09-09,
and the gap left instance names unchecked while the same dot broke them
under `{muxer}` (cluster/solo.py). A DEFAULT the form pre-fills may be bent
legal with `suggested_label` (`my.app` → `my-app`, `café` → `cafe` — NFKD
plus a small table for the letters it cannot decompose; no guessing for
scripts without an ASCII spelling); a name the user typed never is —
refuse it with the reason. The docker container-name charset is
the rule's catch-all and lives beside `CONTAINER_NAME_PREFIX` in
`container_probe.py`, where the name format is defined.

### A new helper that's pure (no disk, no third-party)

→ `utils.py`. Domain-neutral helpers only. If it needs disk, it goes in
`file_access.py`. If it needs an identity dataclass, it's a property /
method on that class.

## Anti-patterns

Things to NOT do, with the reason:

- **Don't import `Path` or call `open()` outside `file_access.py`.** The
  chokepoint rule is what keeps disk-quirk handling (sudo fallbacks, atomic
  writes) in one place. New disk reader → new function in `file_access.py`.
- **Don't add a constant `FOO_PATH` outside `paths.py`.** Search returns one
  hit for any path; that's the point.
- **Don't hard-code a tag name in launcher logic when the tag tree can carry
  the fact.** Requirements come from tree nesting; container config from
  `tag.docker`; copy from `tag.info`. An `if name == "code"` in orchestration
  is a smell — the handful of legitimate ones (cache mounts, the whitelist
  plant) live in `tag_handlers` / `user_additions` and say why.
- **Don't add a free function that takes an `Instance` and computes one of
  its fields.** That's a `@property` on the dataclass — same call site
  shape, lives next to the rest of the identity surface.
- **Don't teach any module outside `tags/migrations.py` about retired
  formats.** Format knowledge ages out in one place.
- **Don't introduce a new cycle (even a lazy one).** The existing `network
  ↔ docker_config` cycle is a documented exception; new ones get pushed to
  a leaf-er module first.
- **Don't add a `# noqa` without naming the rule.** `# noqa: E402` not bare
  `# noqa`. Same for `# type: ignore[misc]` over bare `# type: ignore`.
- **Don't re-export by importing-and-not-using.** If module A needs `X` and
  `X` lives in module C, import directly: `from .C import X`. Routing it
  through B (`from .B import X`) is a re-export hop with no value.
- **Don't write raw `subprocess.run([cmd, ...], capture_output=True, text=True)`.**
  Use `utils.shell_capture(*cmd)` — same shape, less noise, signals "this is a
  read-only probe of a CLI tool." Every read-only docker/getent probe routes
  through it. Reserve direct `subprocess.run` / `subprocess.call` for cases
  that genuinely need extra kwargs (`timeout=`, `env=`, `check=True`).
- **Functions that exist to prompt the user start with `prompt_`.** `prompt_keypress`,
  `prompt_install_failures`, `prompt_tags`, `prompt_session` — name reflects
  the side-effect shape. Don't pick a name that reads like a getter
  (`collect_*`, `check_*`, `get_*`) when the function returns `None` and
  exists for the user-facing prompt.
